{
  "_README": [
    "TEMPLATE 7 — ARTICLE  (/resources/itad-circular-economy)",
    "",
    "=========================================================================",
    "WHEN TO USE Article — and why only ONE of your two resource pages qualifies",
    "=========================================================================",
    "",
    "Article describes EDITORIAL content: something written, by someone, on a date, that",
    "teaches or argues. It is not a wrapper you add to make a page look authoritative.",
    "",
    "Google's structured data policy requires markup to match the page. Their guidance is",
    "explicit that Article does not belong on service pages, product pages, category pages or",
    "marketing landing pages, and misrepresenting page type can draw a manual action.",
    "",
    "I read both of your resource pages. They are not the same kind of page:",
    "",
    "  /resources/itad-circular-economy   1,405 words in <main>.  ARTICLE. Ship this template.",
    "     Genuinely editorial. It explains IEEE 2883 and media sanitization standards, argues",
    "     that physical destruction alone is often not the secure choice, walks through GHG",
    "     accounting for circular models, and compares emissions impact of reuse vs recycling.",
    "     It teaches. It has a thesis. That is an article.",
    "",
    "  /resources/reporting-esg           524 words in <main>.  NOT AN ARTICLE.",
    "     Read the H2s: 'ITAD Recorded Sustainability Metrics' and 'What Every Reporting",
    "     Engagement Delivers'. The body says 'CAS organizes the reporting so...' and lists what",
    "     a client receives. That is a SERVICE description wearing a resources URL. Marking it",
    "     Article would misrepresent it, and at 524 words it would be a thin one.",
    "     -> Mark it up as Service + WebPage instead (template 3 pattern), OR rewrite it into a",
    "        real article about ESG reporting for ITAD — what Scope 3 category 5 actually",
    "        requires, how the EPA WARM model works, where avoided-emissions claims break down —",
    "        and then apply this template. Do not just relabel it.",
    "",
    "=========================================================================",
    "REQUIREMENTS",
    "=========================================================================",
    "Google states there are NO strictly required properties for Article. But for rich result",
    "eligibility you should treat these three as required: headline, image, datePublished.",
    "Strongly recommended: author, dateModified, publisher, description.",
    "",
    "=========================================================================",
    "SET EXPECTATIONS ON THE PAYOFF",
    "=========================================================================",
    "The Article rich result mainly powers Top Stories and Discover, both news-oriented. A B2B",
    "ITAD resource page will realistically get a date in the snippet and little else visually.",
    "",
    "That is not the reason to do it. The reason is author attribution and freshness dates,",
    "which are real E-E-A-T inputs, and clean authorship is one of the strongest signals",
    "available for AI Overview and LLM citation. For a company selling regulatory compliance,",
    "a named expert with verifiable credentials attached to technical content is worth more",
    "than the rich result would have been.",
    "",
    "Strip all _README and _comment keys before shipping."
  ],

