      "_comment_headline": "Keep under 110 characters and match the visible H1. Your H1 is 'Data Sanitization & the Circular Economy' — 40 characters, good.",
      "headline": "Data Sanitization & the Circular Economy",

      "description": "How NIST 800-88 and IEEE 2883 sanitization unlock reuse for enterprise storage media, and what that means for GHG accounting and circular business models.",

      "image": { "@id": "https://core-asset-sol.com/resources/itad-circular-economy#primaryimage" },

      "_comment_dates": [
        "Both dates are ISO 8601 with a timezone offset. Include the offset — without it Google",
        "assumes Googlebot's timezone.",
        "",
        "datePublished must be the real first-publication date. dateModified must be the real",
        "last substantive edit. Do NOT bump dateModified on every deploy to fake freshness — an",
        "accurate date is a useful signal, an inflated one is a liability, and Google compares",
        "the claim against what actually changed.",
        "",
        "This page currently has neither date anywhere in the markup or the visible content.",
        "Show them on the page too — the markup and the page must agree."
      ],
      "datePublished": "REPLACE_WITH_REAL_ISO_DATETIME_WITH_OFFSET",
      "dateModified": "REPLACE_WITH_REAL_ISO_DATETIME_WITH_OFFSET",

      "_comment_author": [
        "=====================================================================",
        "THE AUTHOR PATTERN — this is where template 6 pays off",
        "=====================================================================",
        "A bare @id reference to the Person node defined on /about/team. Nothing is duplicated",
        "here: name, bio, LinkedIn, credentials all live in one place, and when Will's title or",
        "credential changes you edit one file.",
        "",
        "Google's Article documentation recommends author.url point at a page that uniquely",
        "identifies the author, and says that if it is an internal page you should mark that page",
        "up with profile page structured data. So the progression is:",
        "   now:   author -> @id on /about/team#will-gibson",
        "   later: build /about/team/will-gibson as a real ProfilePage and repoint url there",
        "",
        "CRITICAL: the author must be a real named person who actually wrote this. Do not",
        "attribute it to the company to avoid naming someone — 'author: Core Asset Solutions' is",
        "legal schema but throws away the entire E-E-A-T benefit, which is the only reason to",
        "add Article markup to a page like this."
      ],
      "author": { "@id": "https://core-asset-sol.com/about/team#will-gibson" },

